  "source": "WhisperX word boundaries; interpolated character positions",
  "pronunciationText": "Now squash it. In e to the two x, x gets doubled before the exponential acts. So the new curve reaches every height in half the sideways distance. Watch this tangent triangle. It runs across one unit and rises about two point seven. Squash the curve, and the triangle squashes with it. The rise stays the same, but the run halves. Same rise, half the run, so the slope is twice as steep. The whole curve squashed evenly, so that's true at every point. At height one, the slope is now two. The gradient is always two times the height, so the derivative of e to the two x is two e to the two x."
